A tiny TCP/IP stack for Z80
===========================
Or calling it a TCPIP stack might be a stretch.
It is a half-duplex stack that under favorable conditions
has some degree of interoperability with a TCPIP
peer.
Half-duplex as in it can either receive data or it can
transmit data but not both at the same time and using a single
shared buffer for both RX and TX.

(If your stack is picky about the peer playing games with the advertized
 window you are not going to have a good time talking to this stack.
 But it is small, so there is that.)
 

It is designed to be as small as possible but still capable to
exchange data with a TCP endpoint.
It is designed for use-case where you send a small amount of data,
that fits inside a single TCP segment, to the server and then
wait for and receive a small amount of data, that also must fit inside
a single TCP segment, coming back from the server.

What does it support:
* A single tcp connection.

* A single static buffer for both sending packets as well as receiving
packets coming back.

* Retransmitting after ~1 second IF we sent data but did not receive an ACK.

* Detecting and ignoring when the other side retransmits data.
  (Which happens pretty often as we often are too slow to ACK data quickly
   enough causing the server to retransmit.)
   
* A very small footprint.


Files
=====
slip.c slip.h   # Send and receive IP packets over RS232
ip.c ip.h       # Craft IP heaer for sending packets
icmp.c icmp.h   # If you want to send icmp packets, otherwise skip these
tcp.c tcp.h     # "tcp" layer for sending/receiving data on a "tcp socket"

slip-z80.asm    # The same three layers written by hand in Z80 assembler.
ip-z80.asm      # Optional, see below.
tcp-z80.asm


Z80_ASM=1
=========
Hand written Z80 assembler versions of the slip, ip and tcp layers are
included. They are drop-in replacements - same entry points, same calling
conventions - and are built instead of the compiler output with :

  make Z80_ASM=1

They are the same code, just encoded better than sccz80 manages :

                 compiled      asm     saved
  ip.o                 463      240      -223
  slip.o               419      277      -142   (+9 bytes of bss)
  tcp.o               2004     1163      -841   (+14 bytes of bss)
                      ----     ----     -----
                      2886     1680     -1206

A whole program shrinks by rather more than that, around 1500 bytes,
because the compiled version also pulls in library helpers that nothing
else needs - the long arithmetic routines, htonl, memcmp and the stack
access helpers. For example tiny-irc.bin, which has to fit in the ~8.7K a
16K Spectrum leaves free, drops from 8580 to 7106 bytes.

The .c files remain the specification. Each pair is checked against the
other with :

  make test-z80

which builds both, runs them under the z80 simulator that comes with
z88dk and diffs the bytes they put on the wire and the values they
return. See README.tcp-z80 for the details and for the individual
Z80_ASM_IP / Z80_ASM_SLIP / Z80_ASM_TCP switches.


FUSE Spectrum Interface1 bug
============================
FUSE emulation of Interface 1 RS-232 seems to treat the value 0x00
as an escape character:
https://sourceforge.net/p/fuse-emulator/bugs/514/#27de
thus we have to escape 0x00 in our SLIP implementation when building
to run on FUSE.

This means we are no longer compatible with the standard SLIP protocol
and we need to use slip-tun with the corresponding workaround.


SLIP
====
Z80 machines do not come with network interfaces so
this stack is built ontop of SLIP.
SLIP is used to talk between the Z80 host and a
linux host running a special slip endpoint (slip-tun.c)
that bridges the packets between the RS232 port
on the Z80 machine and the outside world.

The implementation of SLIP differs to RFC1055
in that this implementation also ESCapes the 0x00
character, a character which for example the Interface1
on Spectrum appears to have issues with.
Thus this is not compatible with RFC1055 endpoint
but can only be used with slip-tun.c which contains the
same enhancement to the SLIP protocol.

Slip is implemented in slip.c.


-DSLIP_ESC_00
=============
Use this define when compiling for FUSE Spectrum + Interface1.
Both the stack itself as well as slip-tun.c
This adds the special "escape the 0x00 characters" we need on FUSE.


slip-tun
========
slip-tun is a small utility that can act as a network bridge
between a tun interface and the rx/tx channels from the
spectrum serial port.

When running on an emulated system (FUSE, Spectrum48k + Interface1)
this would be two fifos, one for RX and one for TX to the spectrum.
For a physical spectrum48k, this would be the serial port where
the speccy is connected to the host.

Usage:
slip-tun [-v] <tun> <rx> <tx>

<rx> and <tx> are named from the spectrums point of view, i.e. the
spectrum reads from <rx> and writes to <tx>.
If they do not already exist they are created as fifos.
-v logs every packet that is bridged in either direction.

Example:
--------
Use NET.sh rather than invoking slip-tun by hand. It builds slip-tun with
the right defines, starts it on tun0 with /tmp/rx and /tmp/tx, and - the
part you cannot do by running slip-tun directly - sets up the host so the
spectrum can reach anything other than 192.0.2.1. See "Reaching the outside
world" below.

# In first console :
./NET.sh          # or ./NET.sh -v to log every packet

# In a second console
fuse --rs232-tx=/tmp/tx --rs232-rx=/tmp/rx

NET.sh needs sudo, both for slip-tun itself and for the firewall rules. It
removes the rules again when it exits, so stop it with ^C rather than
killing it, otherwise they are left behind. Re-running it is harmless
either way, the rules are only added if they are not already there.


Addressing
----------
slip-tun brings the tun interface up and gives it the address
192.0.2.1/24. The spectrum at the other end of the serial line is
192.0.2.2, and this is the address the stack on the spectrum should
use as its source address.
Packets that arrive on the tun interface are only forwarded to the
spectrum if they are IPv4 and addressed to 192.0.2.2. Packets coming
back from the spectrum are injected onto the real network using a raw
socket, which is why slip-tun needs to run as root.


Reaching the outside world
--------------------------
Out of the box the spectrum can only talk to 192.0.2.1, the host end of
the link. 192.0.2.0/24 is TEST-NET-1 and nothing on the internet will
route a reply back to it, so to reach anything else the host has to
forward and masquerade for us. slip-tun does not do this itself, it only
brings the interface up - NET.sh is what installs the rules :

  sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1
  iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -s 192.0.2.0/24 ! -o tun0 -j MASQUERADE
  iptables -I FORWARD 1 -o tun0 -j ACCEPT
  iptables -I FORWARD 1 -i tun0 -j ACCEPT

The two directions are not symmetric, which is worth knowing if you ever
have to debug this :

Packets from the spectrum are not routed at all. slip-tun re-injects them
with a raw IP_HDRINCL socket, so as far as the kernel is concerned they
are locally generated and they only traverse OUTPUT and POSTROUTING, never
FORWARD. POSTROUTING still sees them, which is why MASQUERADE works, but
a FORWARD rule on its own would do nothing for this direction.

The replies do take the normal path. They arrive on the uplink addressed
to the host, conntrack undoes the masquerade back to 192.0.2.2, and the
kernel then forwards them out tun0 - so the "-o tun0" FORWARD rule is the
one that actually matters. The "-i tun0" rule is not needed today but is
there in case the raw socket is ever replaced by plain routing.

The FORWARD rules are inserted at the head of the chain so they win over
any DROP that docker or libvirt may have added. Note that netfilter
requires every hooked chain to accept: if pings out still fail, check
whether some other nftables table has a forward chain with "policy drop",
because an ACCEPT in the filter table cannot override that.


Attaching and detaching
-----------------------
slip-tun treats the fifos as a session. FUSE opening the <rx> fifo for
reading is what slip-tun considers a client attaching, and it logs
"Client attached" when this happens.

While no client is attached slip-tun does not write anything into the
fifos. Packets that arrive on the tun interface in the meantime are
read and dropped.

When the client detaches, both fifos are emptied.



API
===
Before TCPIP can be used the RS232 SLIP session must be
configured. This is done by calling :

int slip_init(int baud_rate, int parity);
-----------------------------------------

TCPIP
=====
A minimal implementation of "tcpip" is in the files
ip.c
icmp.c
tcp.c

This is a very minimal implementation, designed for small
size and not feature set.
There is no sockets API.
Instead there are four special functions to manage the TCP connection.
The stack only supports a single TCP connection.


-DRS232_TPS=xyz
---------------
If there is no data to read rs232_get() will eventually timeout.
We need to know how long this timeout is so that we can detect when we need
to do a retransmission or when the application wants to abort the TCP
operation. This define specifies how many timedout calls to rs232_get() per
second and this will be different for every rs232 implementation.

For Spectrum Interface1 this should be set to 80.
I.e. -DRS232_TPS=80

For other serial ports you will need to etermine what this value should be.
(It does not have to be very accurate.)

The rs232_get() timeout is basically what we use as a very very crude
clock/timer.

int tcp_connect(tcp_context_t *tcp);
------------------------------------
@ tcp : Must be initialized :
  tcp->ip.saddr : source ip address
  tcp->ip.daddr : destination ip address
  tcp->src_port : source tcp port in network byte order
  tcp->dst_port : destination tcp port in network byte order
Returns :
 0 : Success. Tcp connection is established.
<0 : Failure.

This function is used to establish a TCP connection between src/src_port and
dst/dst_port.
If the attempt fails, for example if it collides with and older session on
the server, it will re-attempt the connection a handful of times
with a random src_port.



int tcp_send(tcp_context_t *tcp, int len, uint8_t *data, int window);
@ tcp  : Must be connected by tcp_connect()
@ len  : Length of tcp payload to send.
@ data : Data to send. Can be NULL if application already wrote the data
         to the tcp buffer.
@ window : Advertized window. Will generally be 0 unless we are ready to
         receive data
Returns :
 0 : Success. All data sent and was ACKed by the peer.
<0 : Failure.
-------------------------------------
Send len amount of data to the server and wait for the server to ACK the data.
Will retransmit the data a few times if the data has not been ACKed after ~1s.

Data can be NULL, for example if the application has already written
the PDU directly into the tcp buffer.

One caveat on that. If tcp_send() has to retransmit it re-sends whatever
is in the packet buffer, and recv_packet() has been writing incoming
packets into the front of that same buffer while we waited for the ACK.
A data argument is copied in again on the way round so it survives; a
payload written into tcp_tx_buffer() cannot be, so tcp_send() returns -1
rather than retransmit one. Such a caller gets a single attempt and has
to rebuild and resend for itself; pass data if you want the retries.

With the window closed the only thing that can arrive while tcp_send()
waits is an ACK, so a request/response protocol never sees this. IRC is
the outlier: the server talks unsolicited.

Window is the advertized window to send. It will generally be 0 when sending
data or a pure ACK.
Window>0 is used temporarily during receival of data in tcp_recv()




int tcp_recv(tcp_context_t *tcp, int len);
@ tcp  : Must be connected by tcp_connect()
@ len  : Maximum amount of data to receive.
         Can be 0 if we just want to wait for an ACK.
Returns:
>0: Amount of data received for this TCP sesison.
 0: Something was received but it it did not contain data
    for this session. It could have been an ACK.
-EAGAIN: Timed out waiting for a reply or we did not receive an
    ACK for the full amount of data. Try again.
<0: Some other error.
-------------------
This reads and populates the network buffer with the next packet of
TCP payload data.
If no packet has arrived after ~1s the function will timeout with -EAGAIN.



int tcp_recv_timeout(tcp_context_t *tcp, int len, int to);
@ tcp  : Must be connected by tcp_connect()
@ len  : Maximum amount of data to receive.
@ to   : How long to wait for a packet, counted in rs232_get() timeouts.
         tcp_recv() is just this one called with RS232_TPS, i.e. ~1s.
         Note that recv_packet() performs to-1 reads so a value below 2
         does not read anything at all.
Returns: same as tcp_recv().
-------------------
Same as tcp_recv() but with an explicit timeout.

This is for applications that have something else to do than to sit in
the serial port for a whole second at a time, for example a chat client
that has to read a keyboard as well (see tcp_chat.c). Such an
application polls with a short timeout and keeps a window open towards
the peer between the polls, since the peer will not send us anything
while the window is closed.

Note that the serial port has no receive buffer, so anything that
arrives while we are not inside rs232_get() is lost. An application that
polls like this should do as little as possible between the polls and
rely on the peer retransmitting what we missed.



uint8_t *tcp_rx_buffer(tcp_context_t *tcp);
--------------------------
This function returns a pointer to the received payload.

uint8_t *tcp_tx_buffer(tcp_context_t *tcp);
--------------------------
This function returns a pointer to the transmit payload buffer for
applications that want to write the data straight into the buffer instead
of passing it as a pointer to data in tcp_send().



Timings/Timeouts
================
On FUSE Interface1, if there is no data to read then each rs232_get() takes
~12ms. This can be used as a timeout to break out of the loop in tcp_recv()
if there is no data to receive.
We possibly need to check how long rs232_get() for an idle serial port takes
for all other interfaces as well.




EXAMPLE UTILS
=============

ping
====
Ping is a simple tool to test if a host is reachable.
You type in the ip address of the host and then the tool
tells you if it is alive or not.


tcp_chat
========
tcp_chat.c is a chat client. Point it at a
        ncat --chat -l <port>
on the linux side of slip-tun and you can talk to whoever else is
connected to it. Any other line oriented tcp service works too.

It asks for the ip address and the port of the server, connects, and then
uses the bottom line of the screen as the line you type on and all the
lines above it as the chat log.

Keys:
  ENTER           send the line
  CAPS SHIFT + 0  erase the last character (CAPS SHIFT + 5 also works)
  CAPS SHIFT + SYM SHIFT + C   quit

Typing and receiving at the same time
-------------------------------------
There is no OS, no threads and no interrupts we can use here, so both
things are done from one single polling loop.

The keyboard is read with in_GetKey() which scans the keyboard ports
directly. We can not use scanf()/fgetc_cons() while connected because
those read the ROMs LAST-K system variable, and LAST-K is only updated by
the 50Hz interrupt routine. rs232_get() runs with interrupts disabled for
most of the time we are connected, so those interrupts almost never
happen and LAST-K is as good as dead.
in_GetKey() counts debounce and key repeat in "number of calls", so the
in_Key* variables are tuned for the ~40 calls per second that the loop
ends up doing.

The connection is polled with tcp_recv_timeout() using a timeout of a
couple of rs232_get() timeouts (~25ms) instead of the ~1s that tcp_recv()
would wait, so that we get back to the keyboard often enough to not miss
a keypress.

Unlike the rest of the stack, which keeps the window closed and only
opens it for the duration of one tcp_recv(), a chat client has to be
ready to receive at any time. So it keeps a window open while idle and
re-opens it every time the stack has closed it again. Since the serial
port has no receive buffer this only works because the loop spends
almost all of its time inside rs232_get(); whatever arrives in the gaps
is lost and is retransmitted by the peer.

The screen
----------
The z88dk console driver scrolls the whole screen when a newline is
printed on the last row and it has no notion of a scrolling region. So a
line is added to the chat log by printing it on the input row and
following it with a newline, which lifts it up into the log and leaves
the bottom row free for the input line to be redrawn on.

Note that the +zx console is 64 columns wide by default (the 4x8 font).
Building with "#pragma output CLIB_ZX_CONIO32 = 1" gives the normal 32
column screen instead, in which case SCREEN_COLS in tcp_chat.c has to be
changed to match.


Tests
=====
The screen and input handling in tcp_chat.c can be tested on the host:

        make test

test/test_screen.c emulates the z88dk console driver (the same control
codes, wrapping and scrolling) and #includes tcp_chat.c so that the real
code is what is being tested. The stubs it needs for the z88dk headers
are in test/stub/.

This does not test any of the networking, only that what ends up on the
screen is what we intended.

The stack itself is tested with :

        make test-z80

See the Z80_ASM=1 section above.
